const String pageResponses = r'''# Responses and resources

Handlers can return `Response.json(data, status: 201)`, `Response.text`,
`Response.html`, `Response.redirect`, `Response.stream`,
`Response.noContent()`, any `JsonSerializable` or `Responsable`, maps,
lists, models and paginators. Dates serialize like Carbon's `toJSON()`:
UTC with microseconds, `2026-01-02T03:04:05.000000Z`.

`Response.json(data, phpCompatible: true)` writes the bytes of PHP's
`json_encode()` instead: `/` as `\/`, non-ASCII characters as `\u`
escapes, and an empty map as `[]` (an empty PHP array). An object that
must stay `{}` when empty, as PHP's `(object) []`, is a
`PhpObject('stdClass', {...})`. Use it when a client compares or signs
the body a Laravel endpoint used to send.

## Cookies

```dart
final theme = request.cookie('theme', 'light');
return Response.json({'theme': theme})
    .withCookie('theme', 'dark', maxAge: const Duration(days: 30))
    .withoutCookie('legacy');
```

`request.cookies` holds every cookie, decoded like PHP's `$_COOKIE`.
`withCookie` adds a `Set-Cookie` header in the format Laravel sends, with
Laravel's defaults (`path=/`, `httponly`, `samesite=lax`); without
`maxAge` the cookie lasts for the browser session. Values are sent as
given: Laravel's `EncryptCookies` middleware has no counterpart yet, so a
cookie Laravel encrypted reads as its encrypted payload.

## API resources

<!-- include: packages/daravel/test/docs/guide_examples_test.dart#resource -->
```dart
final class PostResource extends JsonResource<Post> {
  PostResource(super.resource);

  /// Documents the item for `dartisan openapi:generate` (`.returns(...)`).
  static const Map<String, Object?> schema = {
    'type': 'object',
    'properties': {
      'id': {'type': 'integer'},
      'title': {'type': 'string'},
      'published': {'type': 'boolean'},
    },
  };

  @override
  Map<String, Object?> toArray(Request? request) => {
    'id': resource.id,
    'title': resource.title,
    'published': resource.published,
  };
}
```
<!-- /include -->

- A single resource is wrapped in `{"data": {...}}`.
- `JsonResource.collection(items, PostResource.new)` wraps lists, and for
  paginators produces Laravel's `{data, links: {first, last, prev, next},
  meta}`, where `meta` is the paginator array without `data` and the URLs.
- `when`, `whenLoaded`, `mergeWhen`, `additional({...})` and
  `withMeta(request)` (Laravel's `with()`) are available.

### JSON:API

`JsonApiResource` writes the JSON:API v1.1 shape Laravel 13 writes, with the
`application/vnd.api+json` content type.

```dart
final class PostResource extends JsonApiResource<Post> {
  PostResource(super.resource);

  @override
  String toType(Request? request) => 'posts';

  @override
  Map<String, Object?> toArray(Request? request) => {'title': resource.title};

  @override
  Map<String, JsonApiRelationship<Model>> toRelationships(Request? request) => {
    'author': JsonApiRelationship<User>(UserResource.new),
  };
}

router.get('/posts/{post}', (Request request) async {
  return PostResource((await Post.find(request.param('post')))!);
});

router.get('/posts', (Request request) async {
  return JsonApiResource.collection<Post>(
    await Post.query().paginate(perPage: 20),
    PostResource.new,
  );
});
```

`?fields[posts]=title` narrows the attributes, and `?include=author,comments.user`
adds relationships and `included`. A relationship shows only when asked for, and
an unknown name is ignored, as in Laravel. For a resource a route returns, the
kernel loads what the query asks for before it writes the response, a page at a
time. Called anywhere else, what is not loaded is left out.

`toType` has no default, because naming it after the class would need reflection.
`toId` defaults to the model's key. `toLinks` and `toMeta` add those members, and
`JsonApiResource.configure(version: '1.1')` adds `jsonapi`. `maxRelationshipDepth`
limits how deep `include` reaches, 5 by default.

### JSON Schema

`JsonSchema` builds schema documents, like Laravel's `Illuminate\JsonSchema`.

```dart
final schema = JsonSchema.object({
  'name': JsonSchema.string().required().max(255),
  'age': JsonSchema.integer().min(0).nullable(),
  'tags': JsonSchema.array().items(JsonSchema.string()),
}).withoutAdditionalProperties();

schema.toArray();
```

`default` and `enum` are reserved in Dart, so they are `defaultTo` and `enumValues`.

## HTML

`package:daravel/html.dart` builds pages from Dart functions instead of Blade
templates. Text and attribute values are always escaped; markup that must be
written as is goes through `raw()`, like Blade's `{!! !!}`. A node returned
from a handler is sent as `text/html`:

```dart
import 'package:daravel/html.dart';

HtmlNode userCard(User user) => div(classes: 'card', [
  h2([user.name]),                                   // escaped
  p(classes: 'muted', [user.email]),
  if (user.isAdmin) span(classes: 'badge', ['Admin']),
]);

router.get('/users/{id}', (request) async => document(title: 'Profile', [
  mainEl([userCard(await User.query().findOrFail(request.param<int>('id')))]),
]));
```

Children are nodes, strings (escaped text), numbers, `null` (skipped) and
nested lists, so `if` and `for` work inside them. There are helpers for the
common elements (`div`, `span`, `p`, `h1`–`h3`, `a`, `ul`/`ol`/`li`, tables,
`form`, `input`, `label`, `button`, `img`, `meta`, `link`, `br`, `hr`, …),
`el(tag, ...)` for any other, and attributes where `true` writes a bare
attribute (`required`) and `false`/`null` leave it out. Every element helper
takes `id`, `classes` and `attributes`, and `attributes` is written last, so
`link(rel: 'stylesheet', href: ..., attributes: {'integrity': ...})` or
`tbody(id: 'rows', [...])` need no `el()`. It is a separate import because
names such as `a`, `p` and `text` would clash with ordinary variables.
`escapeHtml` (Laravel's `e()`) is exported by both `package:daravel/daravel.dart`
and `package:daravel/html.dart` as one declaration, so a file can import both.

`script(src: '/js/app.js', defer: true)` writes an external script
(`module: true` writes `type="module"`, and `nonce`, `integrity` and
`crossorigin` are parameters too). `script(content: ...)` and `style(css)`
write inline code as it is, not escaped, so it must be trusted code; a
`</script` or `</style` in it is written `<\/script` (`<\/style`) so it cannot
close the element. To hand data to a script, do not interpolate it into
`content`: `jsonScript(data, id: 'props')` writes
`<script type="application/json">` with `<`, `>` and `&` escaped, which the
page reads with `JSON.parse(document.getElementById('props').textContent)`.

### Layouts and components

Blade's `@extends`/`@yield` and `<x-card>` components with slots are plain
functions: the layout takes a parameter for every section it yields, and a
component takes one for every slot (the default slot is `body`):

<!-- include: packages/daravel/test/docs/guide_examples_test.dart#html-layout -->
```dart
/// The site layout (Blade's `@extends('layouts.app')`): every `@yield` and
/// `@stack` is a parameter, and `body` is the default slot.
HtmlDocument appLayout({
  required String title,
  required List<Object?> body,
  List<Object?> head = const [],
  List<Object?> scripts = const [],
  String? csrfToken,
}) => document(
  title: '$title | Example',
  head: [
    link(rel: 'stylesheet', href: '/css/app.css'),
    ...head,
  ],
  // htmx sends these headers with every request from the page.
  bodyAttributes: {
    if (csrfToken != null)
      'hx-headers': jsonEncode({'X-CSRF-TOKEN': csrfToken}),
  },
  [
    header(classes: 'topbar', [
      a(['Home'], href: '/'),
    ]),
    mainEl(id: 'content', body),
    script(src: '/js/htmx.min.js', defer: true),
    ...scripts,
  ],
);

/// A component with named slots (Blade's `<x-card>` with `$title`, the
/// default slot and `<x-slot:actions>`).
Element card({
  required Object? title,
  required List<Object?> body,
  List<Object?>? actions,
}) => article(classes: 'card', [
  h2(classes: 'card-title', [title]),
  div(classes: 'card-body', body),
  if (actions != null) footer(classes: 'card-actions', actions),
]);
```
<!-- /include -->

A page is then `appLayout(title: 'Posts', body: [card(title: ..., body: [...])])`.
`document(bodyAttributes: {...})` sets the attributes of `<body>`.

### htmx

htmx requests send `HX-Request: true`, and `package:daravel/html.dart` reads
it as `request.isHtmx` (with `isHtmxBoosted`, `htmxTarget` and
`htmxTrigger`). A handler answers an htmx request with the fragment to swap
in, any `HtmlNode`, and a browser with the whole page:

<!-- include: packages/daravel/test/docs/guide_examples_test.dart#htmx -->
```dart
Element postRow(Post post) => tr(id: 'post-${post.id}', [
  td([post.title]),
  td([
    if (post.published)
      'Published'
    else
      button(
        ['Publish'],
        attributes: {
          'hx-post': '/posts/${post.id}/publish',
          'hx-target': 'closest tr',
          'hx-swap': 'outerHTML',
        },
      ),
  ]),
]);

void webRoutes(Router router) {
  router.get('/posts', (request) async {
    final rows = tbody(id: 'posts', [
      for (final post in await Post.query().get()) postRow(post),
    ]);
    // htmx asks for the rows only; a browser gets the whole page.
    if (request.isHtmx) {
      return rows;
    }
    return appLayout(
      title: 'Posts',
      body: [
        card(
          title: 'Posts',
          body: [
            table([rows]),
          ],
        ),
      ],
    );
  });

  // The answer is the fragment htmx swaps in: the updated row.
  router.post('/posts/{id}/publish', (request) async {
    final post = await Post.query().findOrFail(request.param<int>('id'));
    await post.update({'published': true});
    return postRow(post);
  });

  // `HX-Redirect` for htmx (which would swap a followed 302 into the
  // target), an ordinary 303 for a plain form.
  router.post('/posts/clear', (request) async {
    await Post.query().delete();
    return htmxRedirect(request, '/posts', status: 303);
  });
}
```
<!-- /include -->

- **Redirects.** The browser follows a `302` inside htmx's request, and htmx
  swaps the page it lands on into the target. `htmxRedirect(request, url)`
  answers an htmx request with `200` and `HX-Redirect: url`, which htmx
  turns into a full page load, and any other request with an ordinary
  redirect (pass `status: 303` after a `PUT`, `PATCH` or `DELETE`).
- **CSRF.** With [sessions](sessions.md#csrf), `POST`, `PUT`, `PATCH` and
  `DELETE` need the token. Put it in `hx-headers` on `<body>`, which htmx
  sends with every request from the page:
  `appLayout(csrfToken: request.session.token, ...)` above writes
  `<body hx-headers='{"X-CSRF-TOKEN": "..."}'>`.

## Caching and compression

Laravel's `cache.headers` middleware sets `Cache-Control`, `ETag` and
`Last-Modified` on successful `GET`/`HEAD` responses and answers
`304 Not Modified` when the client's copy is current:

```dart
router.get('/catalog', catalog).middleware(const [
  'cache.headers:public;max_age=3600;etag',
]);
```

The options are Laravel's (`public`, `private`, `max_age`, `s_maxage`,
`must_revalidate`, `no_cache`, `no_store`, `immutable`, `etag`,
`last_modified=<unix time or date>`, ...) and the headers come out as
Laravel writes them (`Cache-Control: max-age=3600, public`; `private` is
added when neither `public`, `private` nor `s_maxage` is given). `etag`
hashes the body (MD5; Laravel uses xxh128, so the values differ but behave
the same). `If-None-Match` matches exactly, weakly (`W/`), from a list or
with `*`; `If-Modified-Since` is used only without it.

`CompressResponseMiddleware` gzips bodies of at least 1 KB with a text, JSON,
XML, SVG or JavaScript type for clients that send `Accept-Encoding: gzip`
(JSON usually shrinks by 70–90%), sets `Vary: Accept-Encoding` and turns a
strong `ETag` into a weak one. Streams are left alone. Behind a proxy that
already compresses (Caddy's `encode`, nginx's `gzip`), leave it out.

```dart
.withMiddleware((middleware) {
  middleware.use(const [CompressResponseMiddleware()]);
})
```

## Streaming a large JSON array

`Response.streamJson` is Laravel's `response()->streamJson()`: the items of
a stream go out as one JSON array, encoded as they arrive, so a report of
thousands of rows is never held whole in the isolate. The bytes are exactly
what `Response.json` writes for the collected list, so clients need no
change; `wrap: 'data'` puts the array under a key (`{"data":[...]}`) and
`phpCompatible: true` writes PHP's `json_encode()` bytes.
`QueryBuilder.streamJson()` does the same straight from a query, without
building models: it reads the rows in pages of `chunk` (500) like `lazy()`,
so order the query.

```dart
r.get('/users/export', (req) => Response.streamJson(
  DB.table('users').select(['id', 'name', 'email']).orderBy('id').lazy(),
  wrap: 'data',
));

// The same bytes, from the query builder.
r.get('/users/export', (req) => Response.stream(
  DB.table('users').orderBy('id').streamJson(),
  contentType: 'application/json',
));
```

Streaming bounds memory, not CPU: the rows are still decoded and encoded
(1 000 rows of 40 columns took 30 ms streamed against 23 ms for
`get()` plus `Response.json`, AOT, SQLite). It is for responses too large
to hold comfortably, or that a client should start reading before the last
page query has run; paginate when it can.

## Server-sent events and WebSockets

`Response.sse` is Laravel's `response()->eventStream()`: each item of the
stream goes out as soon as it is produced. A plain value is an `update` event
(JSON unless it is a string or number), and an `SseEvent` sets the event
name, `id` and `retry`. When the stream ends, `</stream>` is sent as Laravel
does, or pass `endStreamWith: null` to send nothing.

```dart
r.get('/api/orders/{id}/status', (request) async {
  final id = request.param<int>('id');
  return Response.sse(
    Stream.periodic(const Duration(seconds: 2), (_) => id)
        .asyncMap((id) async => (await Order.find(id))?.toJson())
        .take(30),
  );
});
```

The frames match Laravel's (`event: update`, `data: ...`), so a client
written against either backend works with both. On top of that:

- A `: keep-alive` comment is sent every 15 seconds while the stream is idle
  (`heartbeat:`), so proxies keep the connection open. That write is also how
  a closed tab is noticed, because TCP reports nothing on an idle
  connection. The source stream is then cancelled.
- `x-accel-buffering: no` and unbuffered writes keep nginx and `dart:io`
  from holding events back.
- On shutdown (SIGTERM, `ServerEngine.stop`) open streams end with their end
  event instead of holding the shutdown until its timeout, and `EventSource`
  reconnects to another instance. Read `request.header('last-event-id')` to
  resume from the last `id` it received.
- A client that stops reading (a paused tab, a phone on a bad network) is
  not queued for without limit: the source is read as fast as it produces,
  and once more than `maxBufferedBytes` (1 MiB) wait for the client, the
  stream ends with `retry: 1000` instead of its end event and the source is
  cancelled, so `EventSource` reconnects a second later. Pass
  `maxBufferedBytes: null` to pause the source while the client is behind
  instead, for a generator that honours pause.

`router.ws` upgrades a connection to a WebSocket after the route's
middleware has passed. `auth`, `throttle` and route parameters work as on
any route, and the handler runs in the request's scope, so `Auth.user()`
works there:

```dart
r.ws('/ws/rooms/{room}', (socket, request) async {
  final room = request.param<String>('room');
  socket.add(jsonEncode({'joined': room, 'user': await Auth.id()}));
  socket.listen((message) => socket.add('echo: $message'));
}).middleware(['auth:sanctum']);
```

A plain HTTP request to that path gets `426 Upgrade Required`. On shutdown
open sockets are closed with `1001` (going away). This is a raw WebSocket;
Laravel Echo connects through [Broadcasting](broadcasting.md). Clients that
ask for permessage-deflate get it; `r.ws(path, handler, compress: false)`
turns it off, which saves CPU and memory per connection when one message
goes to many sockets.

The server sees a client leave only while something listens to its
socket. A handler that returns without listening is fine: the server then
listens itself and drops what the client sends. A handler that waits on
`socket.done` without listening never learns that the client left, and
its session lasts until the server stops; listen instead, with
`socket.listen((_) {})` when the messages do not matter. A handler that
writes in a loop should loop while `socket.readyState == WebSocket.open`:
`socket.closeCode` stays `null` until the client's close is read, so
`while (socket.closeCode == null)` never ends in a handler that does not
listen.

What waits to be sent on a WebSocket is counted. A client that reads more
slowly than the server writes made `dart:io` queue every frame in memory;
once more than `maxBufferedBytes` (4 MiB) wait, the queue is dropped and
the socket is closed with `1008` (`slowClientCode:`), and the client
reconnects. Frames added after a socket started closing are dropped instead
of throwing. `realtime_slow_clients_total` (`transport`: `websocket`, `sse`)
counts these clients on `/metrics`.

Both need a real connection. `TestClient` collects a response in full, so
test a finite `Response.sse` with it, and test WebSockets with `ServerEngine`
on port 0 and `WebSocket.connect`.

### Broadcasting to every isolate

With several isolates, the socket of one user is held by one of them, and
the request that has news for that user may run in another. `Broadcast`
publishes to a channel and every isolate delivers to its own listeners,
through the coordinator, without Redis:

```dart
r.ws('/ws/orders/{id}', (socket, request) =>
    Broadcast.pipe(socket, ['orders.${request.param<int>('id')}']));

// Anywhere: a controller, a job, a listener.
await Broadcast.publish('orders.7', {'status': 'shipped'});
```

`pipe` forwards a channel to a socket until it closes. `subscribe` gives
the messages as a stream, for SSE or anything else:

```dart
r.get('/orders/{id}/events', (request) async {
  final updates = await Broadcast.subscribe('orders.${request.param<int>('id')}');
  return Response.sse(updates.messages);
});
```

A message is a string, or anything else as JSON. It reaches only the
isolates that have a listener on the channel, in publish order, and is
not stored: a listener gets what is published after `subscribe` returns.
It stays within one server; for several machines, publish through
something they share, such as a database table or a queue.

For Laravel Echo clients, events go through the Pusher protocol instead
([Broadcasting](broadcasting.md)), which runs on top of these channels.

## Files

```dart
return Response.download('storage/report.xlsx', name: 'Report.xlsx');
return Response.download(tmp, deleteFileAfterSend: true);
return Response.file('storage/manual.pdf', request: request);

return Storage.download('reports/q3.pdf', request: request);
return Storage.disk('s3').response('videos/intro.mp4', request: request);
```

`download` answers an attachment and `file` shows the file in the browser, with
the type from its name, an `ETag` and `Last-Modified`. File names that are not
plain ASCII get `filename*`, and a name with a path or a control character is
an error. A missing file is a 404.

Pass `request` for `Range`: 206 for one range, 416 past the end, as PDF viewers
and iOS players need, and 304 for `If-None-Match`. A request for several ranges
gets the whole file. `deleteFileAfterSend` removes the file once it was sent and
offers no ranges, because a viewer's first small range would delete it.

A disk reads from the start to reach a range, which on S3 means downloading what
comes before it. Do not build the path of `Response.file` from user input; a
disk checks its paths.

### Content made for the request

A file that never exists on disk, built for this request, is downloaded
without writing it first:

```dart
return Response.downloadBytes(utf8.encode(plist), name: 'exam.seb');

return Response.streamDownload(            // Laravel's streamDownload
  DB.table('orders').lazy().map((row) => utf8.encode(csvLine(row))),
  name: 'orders.csv',
);
```

Both take the type from the name (`text/csv`), else
`application/octet-stream`, unless `contentType` says otherwise, and send the
`Content-Disposition` that `contentDisposition(name)` writes (also exported, for
a response built by hand). `downloadBytes` holds the bytes. `streamDownload`
writes the stream as it is produced: a client that goes away cancels it, so an
`async*` producer stops at its next `yield`, and a `HEAD` request gets the
headers without starting it.

## Static files

Laravel leaves `public/` to the web server. A Daravel application is the web
server, so it serves a directory itself:

```dart
Application.configure(basePath: basePath)
    .withStatic('public', prefix: '/assets')
```

```dart
Response.html('<link rel="stylesheet" href="${asset('neo.css')}">');
// <link rel="stylesheet" href="/assets/neo.css?v=1a2b3c4d5e6f">
```

`asset(path)` adds the first 12 hex digits of the file's SHA-256. A request
carrying the current version is answered with
`Cache-Control: public, max-age=31536000, immutable`; without it, or with a
stale one, with `public, max-age=300` (`maxAge:`). `ETag`, 304 and ranges come
from `Response.file`, and no `Content-Disposition` is sent. Types are exact for
what browsers check: `text/javascript`, `text/css`, `application/wasm`,
`image/svg+xml`, `font/woff2`, `font/woff`, `font/ttf`, `font/otf`
(`mimeTypeOf`).

The directory is scanned at boot and a request is answered by looking its path
up among the files found, so no part of the URL reaches the file system: `..`,
`%2e%2e`, `%2f` and backslashes only miss the lookup. Dot files (`.env`, `.git`,
`.vite`) and symbolic links are skipped; the `public/storage` link of
`storage:link` leads to uploads, which a disk serves (`Storage.response`). A
missing directory aborts the boot. Several `withStatic` calls add directories;
`asset` uses the first that has the file, and `prefix: '/'` serves from the root
without hiding your routes.

With `APP_ENV=local` (or `rescan: true`) a new file is found without a restart
and an edited one gets a new version. In production the files found at boot are
all there is: deploy, then restart. Hashing reads every file once at boot (per
isolate); `dartisan static:hash` in the deploy step writes
`public/.static-manifest.json` with each version, size and modification time, and
the boot then reads no file whose size and time still match.

With Vite, `Assets.vite('resources/js/app.js')` (Laravel's `Vite::asset()`) reads
`public/build/manifest.json` (or plain Vite's `.vite/manifest.json`) and gives
`/assets/build/assets/app-4ed993c7.js`; `asset()` does the same for a source the
manifest lists. Built files are named by their hash, so they are always served
as immutable. An entry missing from the manifest throws, as in Laravel.

## Errors

Exceptions are rendered by the exception handler with Laravel's bodies. With
`APP_DEBUG=false` nothing internal leaks:

| Situation | Status | `message` |
| :-- | :-- | :-- |
| Validation failed | 422 | first error + `(and N more errors)`, plus `errors` |
| Not authenticated | 401 | `Unauthenticated.` |
| Gate / policy denied | 403 | `This action is unauthorized.` |
| Unknown route | 404 | `The route api/x could not be found.` |
| `findOrFail` / `firstOrFail` | 404 | `No query results for model [App\Models\Post] 1` |
| Wrong verb | 405 | `The DELETE method is not supported for route ... Supported methods: GET, HEAD.` |
| Body too large | 413 | `The POST data is too large.` |
| Rate limited | 429 | `Too Many Attempts.` |
| `abort(404)` without message | 404 | empty string, like Laravel |
| Anything else | 500 | `Server Error` |

These are Laravel 10 to 13's messages; `COMPAT_LARAVEL_VERSION=8` or `9`
answers with an older Laravel's ([Laravel coexistence](laravel-coexistence.md#older-laravel-versions)).
As in Laravel, every error but 401 and 422 is written pretty-printed (four
spaces, `/` unescaped: `Handler::prepareJsonResponse`), and 401 `Unauthenticated.`
and 422 bodies are compact, so clients that compare bytes see the same body.

Register custom renderers with `withExceptions((e) => e.render<MyError>((error,
request) => Response.json({...}, status: 409)))`. A renderer may be
`async`: it can write an audit row or read a translation before it answers.

## Laravel equivalents

More of Laravel that this works with, and the API to look up on pub.dev.

| Laravel | Daravel | API | Note |
| :-- | :-- | :-- | :-- |
| `$request->replace([...])` | `request.replace({...})` | `Request.replace` |  |
| `$request->float('price') / ->string('name')` | `await request.float('price') / .string('name')` | `Request.float` |  |
| `$request->bearerToken()` | `request.bearerToken()` | `Request.bearerToken` |  |
| `$request->expectsJson()` | `request.expectsJson` | `Request.expectsJson` |  |
| `$request->getRealMethod()` | `request.originalMethod` | `Request.originalMethod` |  |
| `back()` | `Response.back(request)` | `Response.back` |  |
